{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST be the home of drafting, reviewing, and agreeing requirements, the requirement review criteria, the procedure for choosing references, and the criteria the assessment applies to code and tests.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The one marked test, `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_is_home_of_drafting_review_references_and_criteria]`, adequately checks `requirements.cli.skill_structure.requirements`. I did not read the skill files themselves, so the test's phrases are taken as given and I did not confirm they match the shipped text.\n\n**Obligations derived from the requirement**\n1. The `habeas-requirements` skill is the home of drafting, reviewing, and agreeing requirements.\n2. It is the home of the requirement review criteria.\n3. It is the home of the procedure for choosing references.\n4. It is the home of the criteria the assessment applies to code and tests.\n5. \"Home\" comes from the cited parent, `requirements.cli.skill_structure`: each topic is stated in full in exactly one skill. So each topic must be present in `habeas-requirements` and absent from the other skills.\n\n**How the test covers them**\n- **Drafting, reviewing, agreeing (obligation 1):** The test looks through the parsed numbered steps of the Workflow section, using `workflow_steps(\"habeas-requirements\")`. It asserts that some step contains \"Draft. Write drafts\", \"Review. Sort the new and changed requirements\", and \"**Gate:**\". The assertion message names each topic.\n- **Choosing references (obligation 3):** `section(raw, \"## Choosing references\")` must contain \"A *root* is\".\n- **Assessment criteria for code and tests (obligation 4):** `section(raw, \"## What the review checks\")` must contain \"obligations, boundaries, and failure cases no test runs and asserts\" and \"The supervising agent must still check that the code fulfills each applicable requirement\". These are the test-adequacy and code-fulfilment criteria.\n- **Requirement review criteria (obligation 2):** The `## Criteria` section of `references/review-prompt.md` in that skill must contain **Compound:**, **Checkable:** and **One reading:**. This also exercises the rule that a skill includes its `references/` files.\n- **Exactly one home (obligation 5):** `assert_home(\"habeas-requirements\")` runs two checks.\n  - It asserts that the skill's combined text, SKILL.md plus references, contains every marker from `home_markers`. Those markers cover drafting, reviewing, the Gate, the root definition, \"## What the review checks\", \"# Requirement group review\", and the Compound criterion.\n  - It asserts that none of the other four skills contains any of those markers. Failure messages say \"{other} restates {home}'s {marker}\".\n\n**Supporting tests**\nOther marked tests add to this one. `bundled_skills_refer_to_a_topic_home_instead_of_restating_it` and `bundled_skills_direct_the_reference_procedure_and_report_items_without_one` check that other skills only point to the home and do not restate the reference procedure. `bundled_skills_direct_behavior_review_against_requirements` checks the review criteria in more detail. This test does not depend on them.\n\n**Assessment**\n- Each obligation has a concrete assertion on specific text.\n- The absence check against the other skills is enforced.\n- \"Stated in full\" can only be approximated by distinctive marker phrases for a prose requirement like this one. The markers are spread across the topics and sit in the right sections, so a skill that dropped or moved a topic would fail.\n- I found no unmarked test or wrong expectation.\n- No `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply.",
  "references": "f159a23beb16749e13e1d1de7c5be2c78dd6bcfcbb8168129bbd0ec2bacc21bf",
